## SEO

### Keyword research

The keywords were chosen by thinking about what a student, young professional or remote worker in each town would type into Google, then placed in the titles, descriptions, headings and page text. **Before submitting, check each one yourself:** type it into Google and note whether the autocomplete suggests it, and look at "People also ask". Record what you find in the last column.

| Page | Main keywords | Where they appear | Checked in Google autocomplete |
|---|---|---|---|
| Home | coffee shop Middelburg, coffee shop Nelspruit, café Mbombela | Title, description, keyword line above the heading, intro text | _add result_ |
| About | work-friendly café, study café Nelspruit, café with Wi-Fi Middelburg | Title, description, story text | _add result_ |
| Menu | café menu prices, breakfast Nelspruit, coffee and lunch menu | Title, description, section headings | _add result_ |
| Enquiry | group booking café, catering Nelspruit, private event venue Middelburg | Title, description, form options, FAQ | _add result_ |
| Contact | café opening hours Nelspruit, café Middelburg address | Title, description, structured data, branch details | _add result_ |

Both "Nelspruit" and "Mbombela" are used because people search for either name.

### On-page SEO checklist

| Item | What was done | Done |
|---|---|---|
| Keyword research | Table above | ✔ |
| Title tags and meta descriptions | Unique on all five pages, within the recommended lengths | ✔ |
| Header tags | One `<h1>` per page, no skipped levels | ✔ |
| Image optimisation | Descriptive file names, alt text, width and height, compressed | ✔ |
| URL structure | Short, descriptive file names with a canonical link | ✔ |
| Internal linking | Text links between related pages and a shared navigation and footer | ✔ |
| Mobile-friendliness | Responsive layout, viewport tag, hamburger menu | ✔ |

### Off-page SEO plan

These are actions for after the site is live. They are a plan, not something already done.

- **Backlinks:** list both branches in South African business directories (such as Yellow Pages), ask local community pages and food bloggers in Middelburg and Mbombela to feature the café, and approach nearby colleges for a student-discount partnership page that links to the website.
- **Social media:** create Facebook and Instagram pages for Café Queene using the same name, address and phone numbers as the website, add the website link to each profile, and post menu photos weekly. The Open Graph tags make shared links show a photo and a title.
- **Local SEO:** claim a free Google Business Profile for each branch with the same address, phone number and opening hours as the contact page, add photos, and ask regular customers to leave reviews. The structured data on the contact page supports this.

### Additional requests

| Item | What was done |
|---|---|
| `robots.txt` | Allows all crawlers and points to the sitemap |
| `sitemap.xml` | Lists all five pages with a last-changed date |
| Page speed | See the table below |
| Security | See the list below |

**Before going live:** every page, `sitemap.xml` and `robots.txt` contain the placeholder `https://YOUR-SITE-URL/`. After deployment, use Find and Replace across all files in VS Code (Ctrl+Shift+H) to change it to the real address, for example `https://cafequeene.netlify.app/`, and commit the change.

### Page speed

First-load download size measured on a local copy (HTML, CSS, JavaScript and images; Google Fonts and map tiles are not included):

| Page | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Home | 176 KB | 165 KB |
| About | 639 KB | 619 KB |
| Menu | 667 KB | 461 KB |
| Enquiry | 76 KB | 76 KB |
| Contact | 79 KB | 79 KB |

_After deployment, run Google Lighthouse (Chrome: F12, then the Lighthouse tab) on each page and record the Performance, Accessibility, Best Practices and SEO scores here._

### Security

- A Content Security Policy on every page that only allows the scripts, styles, images and connections the site needs (and nothing else), plus a referrer policy. Tested: an injected script and an image from an unknown site were both blocked.
- Integrity hashes on the Leaflet files, so a changed copy on the CDN would be refused.
- No inline styles or inline scripts anywhere in the pages.
- Visitor text is written to the page with `textContent`, never `innerHTML`, so typed text cannot inject code.
- Both forms validate input and contain a hidden spam trap; external links use `rel="noopener noreferrer"`.
- `_headers` adds `X-Frame-Options`, `X-Content-Type-Options` and a permissions policy on Netlify. GitHub Pages ignores this file, so it relies on the security policy inside each page there.
- HTTPS is provided automatically by Netlify and GitHub Pages.

